### Mixed Certificate Chains in the RPKI (actually, why not to mix)
* **Slides**: [Mixed Certificate Chains in the RPKI (actually, why not to mix)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-sidrops-sessb-mixed-certificate-chains-in-the-rpki-actually-why-not-to-mix-01)
* **Presenter**: Job Snijders
* **Details**:
  * Job reversed his previous position advocating for mixed algorithm certificate chains (RFC 9794) within the same RPKI tree, concluding that mixing algorithms is unviable.
  * **Technical Reasons**:
    1. *Security Downgrade*: Non-PQ/RSA certificates in a mixed chain remain vulnerable to quantum attack ("Q-Day").
    2. *Cross-Signing & DoS Risks*: A recently reported DoS vulnerability demonstrated that cross-signing or multi-path certification paths enable denial-of-service attacks against relying party validators. Relying parties must strictly enforce a single issuer per subject to prevent path-building exhaustion attacks.
  * Consequently, cross-signing across algorithms or trust anchors cannot be safely supported, making distinct, single-algorithm trees necessary.
  * **Discussion**:
    * Ties de Kock and Tim Bruijnzeels discussed the distinction between cross-signing and trust anchor key rollovers, noting that separate trees introduce operational complexity for delegated CAs.
    * Bob Beck confirmed X.509 path-building complexities frequently lead to DoS vectors and implementation errors, strongly recommending distinct single-algorithm trees over mixed chains.

